  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nAn upper-level low centered over the southern Manitoba and \nOntario border will track to east of Lake Superior by Wednesday \nevening. Weak mid-level waves rotating around the upper-level \nlow will cross Wisconsin and clip northern Illinois and \nnorthwest Indiana overnight through Wednesday evening. \nMeanwhile, a cold front will cross the forecast area late \nWednesday morning through the afternoon. \n\nThe first mid-level impulse will brush the area to the north \novernight. A reservoir of higher 900 to 750 hPa moisture \nadvecting in from the Missouri River Valley should arrive in \ntime to interact with a N/S axis of low-level isentropic ascent \nand steeper mid-level lapse rates. Expectations are for a broken\nto scattered line of showers with embedded thunderstorms to \ncross northern Illinois overnight through daybreak Wednesday. \nElevated effective shear may be marginally sufficient for a \ncouple stronger storms with small hail.\n\nA lull in precip is expected mid to late morning Wednesday. \nGuidance has come into better agreement with an incoming cold \nfront, with latest trends indicating a much faster frontal \npassage than what was noted just 24 hours ago. The cold front \nlikely will have cleared much of the northwest CWA by late \nmorning, most of the Chicago metro early in the afternoon, and \nnorthwest Indiana mid-afternoon. With deep convection hinging on\ndestabilization via both daytime heating and advection of \nhigher mix-layer dew points ahead of the front, trends suggest \nthat convective initiation may not occur until near or east of a\nValparaiso to Paxton line mid to late afternoon. Conditional on\nthese storm developing before the front clears most of the \nforecast area, deep-layer shear will be favorable for a mix of \nsupercells and merging multicell clusters. Large hail will be a \nthreat with discrete cells while damaging wind is of most \nconcern with any storm.\n\nWith the main mid-level wave trailing the cold front by a few \nhours, have maintained low-end chance PoPs (under 30%) over \nnortheast Illinois mid to late afternoon as the post-frontal \nenvironment may not fully stabilize and/or mix-out. Isolated to \nwidely scattered showers and perhaps a couple narrow but deeper \ncores with lightning are possible during this window.\n\nMid-level ridging over the Canadian Prairies will deamplify \nwhile shifting eastward late this week into the weekend. \nAssociated surface high pressure will track east while becoming \nelongated across the central Great Lakes Thursday and Friday. \nCannot fully rule out sprinkles or weak showers on the lake \nbreeze boundary across northeast Illinois late Thursday \nafternoon, but the subsidence inversion should be strong enough \nto stunt growing diurnal cumulus.\n\nA loosely zonal pattern across North America this weekend into \nearly next week favors a more active storm track north of the \nforecast area, but close enough to warrant periods of low-end \nprecip chances for showers and some thunderstorms as early as \nSaturday night (and especially toward Monday and Tuesday). \nSummer warmth will also return Sunday and possibly Monday with \nincreasing humidity and daytime highs pushing well into the 80s \n(assuming active weather remains bottled up to the north).\n\nKluber",
